  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been run, so there is zero information about which modality, encoder, or profile strategy contributes most to validation accuracy. The entire design space is unexplored. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the simplest possible configuration—a linear PCA encoder applied to a single RNA profile with no image input. It establishes a clean performance baseline for the RNA modality under the most minimal assumptions. 3) Why it is the best first use of budget: As a fast, low-complexity baseline, it anchors every subsequent comparison: (a) swapping the RNA encoder (scvi, scgpt) tells us whether learned representations beat PCA; (b) moving to multiprofile + aggregation tells us whether multi-view profiling adds signal; (c) adding an image branch (image_only or multimodal) tells us whether the image modality contributes beyond RNA alone. Without this reference point, results from more complex configurations would be uninterpretable. Choosing the simplest baseline first maximizes the information gained per experiment under a tight budget."
